        <section><h3>Monte Carlo</h3>
          <p>The whole auction is played ${u(n.runs)} times (seed ${A(n.seed)}). Rules, squads and purses are identical in every run. Each run draws how the whole room rates each player that day (the room rating: one draw shared by all ten teams) on top of each team's own noise; the spread you see is the uncertainty in how the room behaves. For each player six runs are drawn at random and recorded bid by bid, and his lot plays one of them, so the price can land above or below his median as in a live auction. His model value and the 1,000-run numbers stay sealed until the hammer falls; then the result card says how this run compares with the median and the 80% range, and “Show another run” plays the next one.</p></section>
